                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "this is a masterpiece and one of my absolute favorite games, I have never played anything else like it. it is also quite flawed\n\nthe good:\n\nit captures the feeling of classic hacking perfectly. in the age of ai where a lot of devs (myself included) are going through a bit of an identity crisis, this is a very grounding and comforting game. as a puzzle game, way too easy. as a meditative experience to get lost in, best in class\n\nI love the aesthetic, but I'm sure it can be polarizing. either way, the screenshots are accurate\n\nthe rough edges:\n\navoid guides. do not look things up. it's quite easy to ruin entire sections of the game. the progression is driven by you exploring the systems and learning things, not necessarily specific gates. if you get stuck, go explore random systems, make yourself little tools, etc. there's a good chance you'll stumble across a way to progress\n\nin the same vein, there is not much replay value. you can play through again, but you already know enough secrets that the game could be quickly finished. assume there's a hard cap on the time you'll spend with it of about 20 hours\n\nthe biggest problem is the pacing. in particular:\n- there are several days in the beginning before any of the hacking mechanisms are available. you'll wonder if you're playing the wrong game. to make things worse, in my case I missed an event and had to stumble around until I found the step after that (the one part I had to look up in the end)\n- the ending was well done, but the middle part of the game ends too soon. the joy of the game comes from becoming an expert on how things work, and right as I felt like I was getting there it abruptly ends. I wish there was some sort of way to continue in the game after that, even without a goal\n\nI also imagine this would be a bad programming introduction. it does try to introduce concepts gently at first, but I don't know if it'd be enough for some. on the other hand, the tutorials do feel a little patronizing if you don't need them, but they're short and forgettable so not a big deal\n\nif you have the patience to deal with the flaws, this is an amazing experience. it has left a void that no game since has been able to fill",
